\section{Selection}{

\itemize{
\item \code{source = "auto"} first selects canonical coverage explicitly approved in the
registry for the requested family, event, year and view. This takes precedence
over \code{data_dir} and \code{options(swelections.data_dir = ...)}.
Automatic permission requires both the event activation flag and its central
\code{auto_functions} list. Only then is actual manifest coverage checked. A full
or locally configured manifest cannot grant permission for another function.
\item \code{source = "canonical"} explicitly selects a registered canonical reader and
configured/published manifest. Unpublished builds remain available this way.
This explicit choice is independent of auto permission and still requires
the requested asset/view to exist in the selected manifest.
\item \code{source = "local"} uses the user's preserved raw sources, never the network.
\item \code{source = "remote"} uses the supported Valmyndigheten raw route, irrespective
of canonical availability. Unsupported historical remote formats still fail.
}

Without approved canonical coverage, \code{auto} uses a supported remote source,
never user-saved local raw files, even when a raw archive is configured. If
neither source is available, select \code{source = "local"} explicitly for saved
raw files. Remote download caches remain separate from user raw archives.
A missing or incomplete
preliminary source is never replaced by final data.

The release-candidate registry enables RKL 2002--2022, EU 2004--2024 and the
2003 referendum under data-v0.4.1. RKL 2002 excludes candidacies; the referendum
supports results only. EU 2009 excludes automatic preference votes even if a
configured private manifest contains those assets. Use explicit local sources
for that historical preference-vote archive. RKL 2018 selects the corrected
generation, never the former data-v0.1.0 files. Re-elections in 2003, 2011,
2015 and 2019 enable final results/seats only, preserving their actual scope.
These use four manifests such as \code{rkl2011-re-election-manifest.json}.

Each product has a unique manifest filename such as \code{rkl2018-manifest.json},
\code{eu2009-manifest.json} or \code{referendum2003-manifest.json}, alongside the uniquely
named Parquet assets in the data-v0.4.1 GitHub release. Publication is a separate
step: an enabled but not yet published release gives a download error; it does
not silently fall back to raw files. Configured local canonical manifests remain
available for validation; they cannot extend the central auto permission.

Once selected, canonical download, checksum or schema failures are errors, not
reasons to silently change source. Parquet reading requires optional package
\code{nanoparquet}; a missing reader gives installation guidance. SHA256 verification
also requires \code{tools::sha256sum()} on the running R installation.

Mandate-period history uses canonical intervals for 2014/2018 and current
remote member/history exports for 2022. The two date queries filter the same
chair population. 2022 history is not made canonical merely because its fixed
election results are canonical. See \code{\link[=mandate_history]{mandate_history()}} for source coverage.
